    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm putting together a doc for our new CI/CD pipeline. Can you give me a quick breakdown of the standard stages—specifically Code Commit, Automated Testing, Staging, and Production—and what kind of quality gates we should have at each step?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely. In your CI/CD pipeline, **Code Commit** is where developers push code changes to a shared repository. The quality gate here might be successful build or mandatory peer reviews. \n\n**Automated Testing** follows and involves running automated unit, integration, and sometimes end-to-end tests. The quality gate could be all tests passing with at least 80% code coverage.\n\nDuring **Staging**, the main application is deployed for testing in a production-like environment. Quality gates here could be validated environment checks, passing all staging tests, and successful user acceptance testing.\n\nFinally, in the **Production** stage, the changes are deployed to the live production environment. Quality gates may include post-deployment monitoring, zero downtime deployment, and possibly a cool-off period where no further changes are accepted for 72 hours, ensuring stability and reliability.",
      "source": "target",
